--- Part 3: an optional assignment, only if I choose it ---

Signing off with `CA-v1` does not make this part apply, and a sign-off does not
certify it. It applies to a contribution only when I elect it for that contribution
as (f)(1) says. Where it applies, it takes the place of the sentence of (e) that
says I keep the copyright in my contribution; everything else in (e) still applies.
Where it does not apply, (e) alone applies, as before.

(f) Assignment, if I elect it.

    (1) How I elect. I elect this clause by a written statement, signed with my
        name and dated, that names clause (f) of `CA-v1`, identifies the
        contribution (for example by its commit or its pull request) and says
        that I assign it. A statement made in the pull request that carries the
        contribution, under my name, is such a statement. An election covers only
        the contribution it identifies, never anything I create later.

    (2) What I assign. I assign to the operator, free of charge, worldwide and
        for the whole term of protection, the economic rights in that
        contribution: to reproduce it; to distribute it; to adapt, translate and
        otherwise modify it; to combine it with other works; to communicate it to
        the public and make it available online; and to license others to do any
        of these; by any means and in any form known when I elect.

    (3) Licence back. The operator at once grants me a worldwide, royalty-free,
        non-exclusive, perpetual and irrevocable licence to use that contribution
        in every way (2) names, for any purpose, including the right to license
        others. This licence binds anyone who later acquires the rights.

    (4) Where an assignment has no effect. Where the law that applies to me does
        not allow the assignment in (2), or does not give it full effect, I grant
        the operator instead an exclusive licence of the same scope, with the
        right to grant sub-licences, subject to the same licence back as (3).
        Where that too has no effect, I agree not to assert my economic rights in
        that contribution against the operator, or against anyone who receives it
        from the operator under the licences named in the notes below.

    (5) What I keep. This clause concerns economic rights only. My moral rights,
        among them the right to be named as the author and to object to a
        distortion that harms my honour or reputation, stay mine where the law
        does not let them be transferred or waived, and this clause does not try
        to take them. My authorship stays recorded in the item's provenance
        fields and in the repository's history. A right that the law gives me and
        that no contract may exclude is not affected by this clause.

    (6) What the operator promises. The operator keeps every contribution
        assigned under this clause available to the public under terms no
        narrower than those under which this node or repository first published
        it, and may in addition license it under other terms.

    (7) Who the operator is. In this file, "the operator" is the person or
        organisation that publishes the node, or maintains the repository, to
        which the contribution is made, as that node or repository names its
        operator. The word has this meaning in (e) as well.

--- Notes, not part of the agreement ---

The outbound licences are unchanged by this agreement: the engine software is under
Apache-2.0, the schemas, the ontology, the identifiers and the discovery document
are under CC0-1.0, and the published prose is under the Content Use Terms
(`LICENSE-CONTENT`), whose clause 5 this agreement supplies the operator grant for.

This file's bytes are pinned by SHA-256 in AGSC-08-06. A distribution that ships
different text MUST name it with a different agreement token, because the token
names terms and not a file name.
